    "updated_at": "2026-02-18T18:51:47Z",
    "body": "You're mistaken, I think. These two assertions produce identical ASTs when parsed by Python:\n\n```pycon\n>>> import ast\n>>> source = '''assert (\n...     # my comment\n...     my_var == 999 if abc == \"TEST\" else 0\n... )'''\n>>> print(ast.dump(ast.parse(source), indent=2))\nModule(\n  body=[\n    Assert(\n      test=IfExp(\n        test=Compare(\n          left=Name(id='abc', ctx=Load()),\n          ops=[\n            Eq()],\n          comparators=[\n            Constant(value='TEST')]),\n        body=Compare(\n          left=Name(id='my_var', ctx=Load()),\n          ops=[\n            Eq()],\n          comparators=[\n            Constant(value=999)]),\n        orelse=Constant(value=0)))])\n>>> source2 = '''assert (\n...     # my comment\n...     my_var == 999\n...     if abc == \"TEST\"\n...     else 0\n... )'''\n>>> print(ast.dump(ast.parse(source2), indent=2))\nModule(\n  body=[\n    Assert(\n      test=IfExp(\n        test=Compare(\n          left=Name(id='abc', ctx=Load()),\n          ops=[\n            Eq()],\n          comparators=[\n            Constant(value='TEST')]),\n        body=Compare(\n          left=Name(id='my_var', ctx=Load()),\n          ops=[\n            Eq()],\n          comparators=[\n            Constant(value=999)]),\n        orelse=Constant(value=0)))])\n>>> ast.dump(ast.parse(source)) == ast.dump(ast.parse(source2))\nTrue\n```",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
